                <head>358. Memorandum From the Chairman of the Working Group of the United States
                    Japan Joint Committee on Trade and Economic Affairs (Yager) to the Working
                        Group<anchor n="0" xml:id="d358-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-10-15">October 15,
                        1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Under Secretary Ball’s Comments on
                        Japanese Trade Restrictions</item>
                    <item>Under Secretary Ball believes that,
                        during the coming Cabinet Committee<anchor n="1" xml:id="d358-f2"/>
                        discussions, we should be much more on the offensive vis-a-vis Japan and its
                        trade restrictions than we have in the past. We should make it clear that if
                        Japan is to be a leading and active participant among the developed nations
                        of the world, it must pursue liberal trade policies. The Under Secretary has
                        noted that the Japanese record of restrictions is in many ways worse than
                        the situation prevailing in the European countries. He hopes that the
                        briefing papers now in preparation will reflect this point of view.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
